acks in the  jewish quarter and in neighboring towns  five years later king pedro of aragon  ordered officials in the inland town of  larida  to provide a new cemetery for the jewish  community because the old one had been  filled with bodies of plague victims  high status in christian society offered  no protection from the disease either  the king of aragon wrote to his uncle  the count of a realm high up in the  pyrenees mountains  to express his condolences at the death  of the count's wife  the count's younger daughter would also  die soon afterwards  the king of aragon himself would survive  but his wife queen leonor would not  in late october he frantically summoned  four physicians from valencia because  leonor was ill  she passed away even before they arrived  from port cities and towns across the  peninsula the plague spread with  frightening speed  historians have often claimed that one  of the epicenters was santiago de  compostela the pilgrimage center to  which thousands of people traveled from  across europe in this period and indeed  today  but in fact the whole western coast the  coast which chaucer's shipment knew like  the back of his weather-beaten hands  was vulnerable  the same was true of the southeastern  coast in particular the city of almeria  one of the main ports of the muslim  kingdom of granada  a medical treatise by the muslim  physician and poet ibn khatimat  suggests that the plague had sunk its  teeth into al-maria by june 1348.  we don't know much yet about the death  rates in seville  the same dazzling city that alfonso the  wise had described in the passage with  which we began this lecture  but we can be sure that those ships  sailing in on the guadalcabia river  would have brought on board infection  and destruction  what exactly was the overall mortality  rate in spain  coastal regions were probably hit harder  than landlocked areas  but evidence from jewish graves suggests  that even places like toledo in the dead  center of 