ews of plague began to spread  the pilgrims found themselves the  targets of suspicion like other  outsiders and ethnic minorities  across europe marginal groups of people  including jews and foreigners  were widely accused of responsibility  for the disease and of having poisoned  the water supplies  many faced verbal and physical abuse  homicide and in the case of the jews  genocide  by mid-summer the pilgrims were in fear  for their lives vulnerable to physical  aggression as well as to the lethal  bacteria  determined to make their way back home  in july 1348  they were given a safe conduct by the  king of aragon pedro the ceremonies  pedro had left valencia when the plague  reached the coast and his court was now  installed some way in land  at this moment the plague was spreading  throughout the entire iberian peninsula  ironically the movement of pilgrims and  royal courts just like the activities of  merchants and sailors accelerated this  diffusion  there were probably multiple  simultaneous points of entry into the  peninsula  we know the plague had arrived in the  northeast in the border counties of  catalonia by april 1348.  the king of aragon wrote to the governor  of the beautiful mediterranean islands  of majorca warning him of the danger  but by this stage it was already too  late  as early as february the governor of  majorca had already sent a letter  ordering officials to inspect all ships  coming from ports in the eastern  mediterranean  he knew what these ships could bring  but his efforts were unsuccessful  on may the 3rd the officials asked the  king to send armed galleys from  barcelona and valencia  because the plague had now struck so  badly that they couldn't defend  themselves against muslim pirates or the  powerful muslim king of tunisia  the plague had an appalling impact on  jewish communities in spain  both directly and because of the  religious violence that it provoked  it reached barcelona by mid-may 1348  sparking anti-semitic attacks in the  jewish quarter and in neighboring towns  five years later king pedro of aragon  ordered officials in the inland town of  larida  to provide a new cemetery for the jewish  community because the old one had been  filled with bodies of plague victims  high status in christian society offered  no protection from the disease either  the king of aragon wrote to his uncle  the count of a realm high up in the  pyrenees mountains  to express his condolences at the death  of the count's wife  the count's younger daughter would also  die soon afterwards  the king of aragon himself would survive  but his wife queen leonor would not  in late october he frantically summoned  four physicians from valencia because  leonor was ill  she passed away even before they arrived  from port cities and towns across the  peninsula the plague spread with  frightening speed  historians have often claimed that one  of the epicenters was santiago de  compostela the pilgrimage center to  which thousands of people traveled from  across europe in this period and indeed  today  but in fact the whole western coast the  coast which chaucer's shipment knew like  the back of his weather-beaten hands  was vulnerable  the same was true of the southeastern  coast in particular the city of almeria  one of the main ports of the muslim  kingdom of granada  a medical treatise by the muslim  physician and poet ibn khatimat  suggests that the plague had sunk its  teeth into al-maria by june 1348.  we don't know much yet about the death  rates in seville  the same dazzling city that alfonso the  wise had described in the passage with  which we began this lecture  but we can be sure that those ships  sailing in on the guadalcabia river  would have brought on board infection  and destruction  what exactly was the overall mortality  rate in spain  coastal regions were probably hit harder  than landlocked areas  but evidence from jewish graves suggests  that even places like toledo in the dead  center of the peninsula were afflicted  terribly  across the plains of castile there was a  major economic shift towards pastoral  farming which didn't require very much  manpower  and in particular the merino wool sector  for navarre a kingdom nestled in the  foothills of the pyrenees  which young geoffrey chaucer was to  visit one mid-winter about 20 years  later  some scholars have proposed a figure as  high as a 50 percent death rate  navarre was a common point of entry for  travellers and for merchants as well as  messengers riding across the mountains  to and from france or pilgrims riding  and walking to santiago  soldiers criss-crossed the high peaks to  participate in both theaters of fighting  in the hundred years war france and  spain  and helped spread disease with them as  they marched  the rate was equally high in catalonia  embedded in networks of mediterranean  trade  historians have pointed to a long-term  decline even a crisis in catalan  commerce in the late 14th and 15th  centuries  here as elsewhere in spain the impacts  of the first wave of plague was  exacerbated by later outbreaks  barcelona which may have had a  population of around 38 000 in the early  1380s  had only half of this number some 20 000  by the middle of the next century  there was a great shortage of laborers  across the region bringing higher wages  for some workers but trouble for others  up in the mountains financially squeezed  landlords began to impose harsher and  harsher conditions on their peasants  one revisionist spanish historian  has challenged this grim picture of the  plague in iberia  for him the impact of the black death  has been exaggerated spain was different  from the rest of western europe he says  but let's take a close look at the  evidence  this historian guillermo castan points  to what he sees as surprising silences  on the part of spanish and portuguese  chroniclers  there's no contemporary narrative that  reflects a sense of demographic  catastrophe he says  although some of these chroniclers do  specify that the plague was present  among the castilian forces besieging  gibraltar  and they reveal that alfonso the 11th of  castile died in 1350 as a result of the  disease while participating in this  siege  the problem is that arguing from silence  in our sources is always  dangerous  there are other texts that suggest a  perception at the time that the plague  was completely universal  in the summer of 1348 for example the  king of aragon rewarded his personal  physician for his services during the  height of the plague which he says had  infected almost the whole world  in a letter to the king of castile  alfonso the 11th in march 1350  shortly before alfonso himself died  he describes how because of the  mortality that by god's will has fallen  on spain and in the same way across the  entire world  all of spain is depopulated  a second dimension to the revisionist  argument  is that the epidemic of 1348 cannot have  been so terrible because it did not  interrupt military campaigning in spain  just as king alfonso the 11th carried on  besieging gibraltar the aragonese were  still able to pursue a policy of  military expansion  in the mid to late 1350s  the war with castile that ensued was to  last well over a decade  but this is again slippery evidence if  we look at the broader european picture  no one doubts that florence for example  was absolutely decimated by the plague  and yet even in 1349-50  while the first wave of disease was  still devastating the city the  florentine authorities also began a new  war against a powerful aristocratic  family  and in 1351 they became involved in a  conflict with the rival states of milan  nowhere was plague an obstacle to war  for very long  the fact that military campaigning  continued is not necessarily a sign that  the plague had only a limited effect  one final factor that's invoked as an  argument that spain cannot have been  that badly affected  is the economic vitality of many parts  of the peninsula in the late 14th  century  in the basque country in northern spain  the appearance of new towns suggests  that this was in fact a period of growth  bilbao emerged as an important export  center  in galicia in the northwest urban life  flourished in the decades after the  black death this was a very prosperous  period for galician port cities like  acoruna  and further south on the atlantic coast  the portuguese city of lisbon was also  thriving  english ships and merchants received  special trade privileges in lisbon in  1367.  chaucer's fictional shipment and his  real-life counterparts would have known  all of these places very well indeed  but economic vitality does not mean that  the black death had avoided spain and  portugal any more than it had avoided  england  we know from our own experience that  pandemic disease may strike at different  times and with different intensities in  the short term  but that in the longer run the impact is  as the king of aragon believed almost  universal  instead what we're seeing is economic  resilience  in the practical world of trade commerce  and sailing the survivors were  rebuilding their worlds  and they were soon to discover new ones  from the little port of dartmouth and  bigger cities like southampton and  london  english shipment was soon plying the  rough seas of the bay of biscay once  again  within a century the coasts of portugal  would become the point of departure for  the earliest global european empire  portuguese mariners navigated their way  down the west african coast  often acting in conjunction with  merchants from venice genoa and florence  bringing back gold cloth and slaves  they sailed into the indian ocean too  where they latched on to vast  pre-existing networks of trade  lisbon was destined to become a great  multi-racial center of colonial trade  and exploitation  the success of these portuguese ventures  would galvanize christopher columbus to  set sail in 1492  from the little spanish ports of palos  de la frontera  initiating a sequence of voyages that  would transport culture and disease  west and east across the atlantic  and seville  the muslim city on the guadalcana river  which had mesmerized alfonso the wise  would become by the 16th century the  greatest commercial metropolis in the  whole of europe  the benefits of this new wealth would be  unevenly shared  there would be poverty as well as  prosperity  but the rebound from the plague which  remained endemic in spain and in europe  was nothing short of astonishing
 

